-- Governance.daml
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE DECENTRALISED OPERATOR — a K-of-N committee that strikes the official
-- price (the NAV / closing fix) so NO SINGLE party can print it alone.
-- ===========================================================================
-- WHY THIS EXISTS
--   A closing/opening auction (MarketOnClose.daml) prints ONE official price and
--   every fill settles at it. That price is only worth trusting if the party who
--   sets it CANNOT set it unilaterally — otherwise the "official fix" is just one
--   venue's opinion and can be manipulated. Real reference-rate administrators
--   (ICE, CME CF, the equity closing auctions) derive their legitimacy from a
--   PROCESS no single participant controls.
--
--   This module models that process natively on Canton as a DECENTRALISED PARTY:
--   a standing `OperatorCommittee` of N member parties with a threshold K, and a
--   fixing that only becomes official once K DISTINCT members have attested to it.
--   It is the self-contained analogue of BitSafe / DLC.Link's "Decentralized Party
--   Manager" (Apache-2.0), whose attestor nodes act as Canton "signing parties"
--   under threshold governance — reimplemented here at the CONTRACT level so it
--   compiles on the bare SDK (no external governance .dar), exactly as this project
--   reimplements Daml Finance's holding/settlement shapes.
--
-- THE AUTHORISATION PATTERN (accumulating multi-signature)
--   Daml gives a party's authority to a choice body iff the party signs the
--   contract being exercised or controls the choice. Authority does NOT persist
--   across transactions. So a K-of-N attestation is built the canonical way:
--     1. ProposeFixing — a member proposes a price. The proposal is signed by that
--        one proposer; every other member observes it.
--     2. Confirm       — each additional member confirms. Confirm ARCHIVES and
--        RE-CREATES the proposal with the confirming member added to BOTH the
--        approver list AND the signatory set. The confirming member controls the
--        choice (its authority is present to add itself as a signatory); the
--        existing signatories' authority is present because they signed the
--        contract being exercised. The signature set thus GROWS one member per tx.
--     3. FinalizeFixing — once >= K approvers have signed, any approver finalises:
--        it creates a `NavFixing` whose signatory set IS all the approvers. Because
--        the proposal already carries all their authority, the fixing can be signed
--        by all of them in one step. A `NavFixing` therefore CANNOT EXIST without K
--        genuine member signatures — Test:testThresholdAttestation proves a single
--        member cannot finalise a 2-of-3 fix.
--
-- WHY CANTON / DAML FITS
--   The attestation is a first-class ledger fact: the `NavFixing`'s signatories ARE
--   the attestors, so "this price was struck by K of N" is provable by inspecting
--   the contract, not asserted by a server. On a transparent chain you would model
--   this as a multisig contract too, but the PRICE and the members' participation
--   would leak to the whole world; here the resting proposal is visible only to the
--   committee (+ auditor), and the published fix is disclosed to exactly the venues
--   that consume it. Governance WITHOUT leakage — the same Canton property the
--   auction and the DvP rely on.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- CONTINUOUS ACCRUAL — THE COMMITTEE ATTESTS THE INPUTS, THE LEDGER COMPUTES.
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE GAP THIS CLOSES. Everything above produces a SNAPSHOT: a price, struck by K of
-- N, true at one instant. That is how a fund has been valued since funds existed,
-- because a human process could not do better than once a day. But a tokenised fund
-- trades every second of every day, and between two fixings the old design has NO
-- VALUE AT ALL — a share is worth the last mark until somebody strikes a new one,
-- which is wrong every second in between and gets more wrong the longer the gap.
--
-- THE INSIGHT, AND IT IS THE WHOLE PRODUCT STORY: A TREASURY OR MONEY-MARKET FUND'S
-- VALUE BETWEEN MARKS IS NOT DISCOVERED — IT IS ACCRUED. There is no price discovery
-- happening between 16:00 and 16:00. A T-bill does not trade its way to tomorrow's
-- value; it EARNS its way there, at a rate that was already agreed, on a day-count
-- convention that was already agreed. Yield accrues per second. It is ARITHMETIC. No
-- oracle, no market, no counterparty, no discovery.
--
-- SO SPLIT THE PROBLEM ALONG THE LINE THE ECONOMICS ALREADY DRAWS:
--
--     THE COMMITTEE ATTESTS THE INPUTS. THE LEDGER COMPUTES THE ACCRUAL.
--
--   Humans agree only on what cannot be derived — the base NAV, the rate per annum,
--   the day-count convention, and the instant the mark applies from. Four numbers, no
--   more. EVERYTHING DERIVABLE IS DERIVED ON-LEDGER, deterministically, by every
--   validator that re-executes the transaction, at whatever instant it is asked.
--   That is the cleanest statement of why this belongs on a ledger at all: a ledger
--   is not a database with signatures on it, it is a place where a computation and
--   its authorisation are the SAME artifact. Attesting a number and attesting the
--   FUNCTION THAT PRODUCES EVERY LATER NUMBER are different orders of thing, and only
--   the second one gives you a fund that is correctly valued at 03:41 on a Sunday.
--
--   It also shrinks the trusted surface, which is the security argument. A committee
--   that must re-attest every second has 86,400 chances a day to be wrong, be late,
--   be captured, or be offline. A committee that attests a rate once and lets the
--   ledger integrate it has one. The oracle problem is not solved by faster oracles;
--   it is solved by asking the oracle less.
--
-- WHAT ACCRUES AND WHAT DOES NOT — say this before a judge does. This is right for
-- short-dated, yield-bearing, low-volatility instruments: T-bills, repo, money-market
-- funds, tokenised treasuries. It is WRONG for anything whose value is a market
-- price — equities, cETH, a volatile token — where the value between marks genuinely
-- is discovered and pretending otherwise would be inventing data. The design does not
-- pretend: a fixing with `ratePerAnnum = 0.0` accrues NOTHING and behaves exactly like
-- the snapshot it always was, and that is the DEFAULT the existing `ProposeFixing`
-- path produces. Accrual is opt-in, per fixing, and the committee opts in by
-- attesting a rate.
-- ===========================================================================

module Governance where

import DA.List (dedup)
import DA.Time (subTime, convertRelTimeToMicroseconds)
import DA.Optional (isSome)

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE DAY-COUNT CONVENTION — the input that looks like a detail and is not.
-- ===========================================================================
-- A rate is meaningless without the convention it is quoted on, and the two common
-- ones DISAGREE BY 1.389% ON THE SAME NUMBER. "4%" on ACT/360 accrues 4/360 per day;
-- "4%" on ACT/365F accrues 4/365. Over a year that is 4.056% vs 4.000% — 5.6bp of
-- pure convention, which on a $1bn fund is $560,000. It is precisely the kind of
-- thing a human committee must agree and a ledger must never guess, so it is an
-- ATTESTED FIELD on the fixing rather than a constant in this file.
--
-- THE DEFAULT IS ACT/360, AND HERE IS WHY. Every USD money-market instrument this
-- design is aimed at is quoted ACT/360: SOFR, repo, fed funds, commercial paper, and
-- the Treasury BILL discount rate. If you are accruing a tokenised USD treasury or
-- money-market fund, ACT/360 is not a preference, it is the convention the underlying
-- instrument's own yield was quoted on, and using anything else silently re-prices the
-- fund against its own assets.
--   ACT/365F is supported and is NOT wrong — it is the correct convention for GBP,
--   AUD, NZD, HKD and SGD money markets, and for many swap fixed legs. A sterling
--   money-market fund attesting ACT/365F here is doing the right thing.
--   Deliberately NOT supported: 30/360 and its variants (bond conventions that assume
--   30-day months — meaningless for a per-second accrual), and ACT/ACT (which needs
--   leap-year period boundaries; correct for coupon-bearing Treasury NOTES, but these
--   are SHORT-DATED instruments where the convention is a money-market one and the
--   added machinery would buy accuracy nobody is asking for). Attesting an
--   unsupported string is rejected at proposal time rather than silently defaulted —
--   see `supportedDayCount`.
--
-- SIMPLE INTEREST, NOT COMPOUND, AND THAT IS ALSO A CONVENTION AND NOT AN
-- APPROXIMATION. Money-market yields are quoted as SIMPLE interest over the period;
-- a money-market fund's daily NAV accrual is linear within the accrual period, and
-- compounding happens when the fund DISTRIBUTES and the committee strikes a new base,
-- not continuously inside one mark. So linear accrual from an attested base is the
-- market convention, not a shortcut around a missing exponential. (It also happens to
-- be the only form that is exactly representable here — see PRECISION below — but
-- that is a happy coincidence, not the reason.)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Microseconds in a day. Daml `Time` has microsecond resolution, so microseconds are
-- the natural unit: working in whole seconds would quantise accrual to 1-second steps
-- and throw away resolution the ledger already carries.
microsPerDay : Int
microsPerDay = 86400000000

-- dayCountYearMicros — the length of the convention's YEAR, in microseconds.
-- `None` means "not a convention this venue accrues on", which is how an unsupported
-- string is rejected rather than defaulted.
--   "NONE" is the explicit non-accruing convention. It maps to a year length so the
--   arithmetic stays total, but it is only ever paired with `ratePerAnnum = 0.0`, so
--   the length it maps to can never affect a number.
dayCountYearMicros : Text -> Optional Int
dayCountYearMicros dc
  | dc == "ACT/360"  = Some (360 * microsPerDay)   -- USD money market: SOFR, repo, T-bills
  | dc == "ACT/365F" = Some (365 * microsPerDay)   -- GBP/AUD/NZD/HKD/SGD money market
  | dc == "NONE"     = Some (360 * microsPerDay)   -- non-accruing snapshot (rate is 0)
  | otherwise        = None

supportedDayCount : Text -> Bool
supportedDayCount = isSome . dayCountYearMicros

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- elapsedMicrosFrom — how long this fixing has been accruing, CLAMPED AT ZERO.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE BACKWARDS-TIME GUARD, and it is deliberate rather than defensive. A caller can
-- legitimately ask what a fixing says at an instant BEFORE it applies from — a
-- backfill, a replay, a report run over a window that opens before the mark. The
-- honest answer is "this fixing accrued nothing by then", NOT a negative accrual that
-- would silently mark the fund DOWN below a value the committee actually attested.
-- Clamping keeps the function total and monotone; `navAtChecked` is there for the
-- caller that needs to tell "before the strike" apart from "at the strike".
elapsedMicrosFrom : Time -> Time -> Int
elapsedMicrosFrom from at =
  let micros = convertRelTimeToMicroseconds (subTime at from)
  in if micros <= 0 then 0 else micros

-- ===========================================================================
-- accruedAmount — THE ACCRUAL, as a pure total function. No ledger, no parties.
-- ===========================================================================
--     accrued = basePrice * ratePerAnnum * elapsed / yearLength
--
-- PRECISION — THE PART THAT WOULD QUIETLY DESTROY THIS IF IT WERE WRITTEN THE OBVIOUS
-- WAY. Daml `Decimal` is `Numeric 10`: 38 significant digits, exactly 10 of them
-- after the point. Addition and subtraction are exact; MULTIPLICATION AND DIVISION
-- ROUND TO 10dp. So the ORDER OF OPERATIONS is not a style question here, it is the
-- difference between a correct fund and a broken one.
--
--   THE WRONG WAY, written out so nobody re-introduces it: compute a per-second rate
--   first. At 4%/yr ACT/360 that is 0.04 / 31,104,000 = 1.28600823e-9, which at 10dp
--   rounds to 0.0000000013 — a 1.09% RELATIVE ERROR baked into every second, forever.
--   Anything that divides before it multiplies produces a fund that is wrong by more
--   than a percent of its own yield.
--
--   THE ORDER USED BELOW: `basePrice * (ratePerAnnum * elapsedMicros) / yearMicros`.
--     * `ratePerAnnum * intToDecimal elapsedMicros` is EXACT — a 10dp value times a
--       whole number of microseconds has at most 10dp, so nothing is lost.
--     * multiplying that by `basePrice` is the only intermediate rounding, and it is
--       bounded by half a unit in the last place: 5e-11 ABSOLUTE, on a quantity of
--       order (base x rate x micros).
--     * dividing by the year length (3.11e13) then SHRINKS that inherited error by
--       thirteen orders of magnitude, to ~1.6e-24 — invisible.
--     * the division itself rounds once, to within 5e-11 of the true accrual.
--   NET: ONE evaluation of this function is within ~5e-11 (half a ulp) of the exact
--   real-number answer. That is the tightest bound a 10dp fixed-point type admits,
--   and it is a bound rather than an estimate.
--
--   HEADROOM, STATED SO IT CAN BE CHECKED: the largest intermediate is
--   `basePrice x ratePerAnnum x elapsedMicros`, which must stay inside 38 digits with
--   10 reserved for the fraction, i.e. below 1e28. A $1,000,000-per-share NAV at 10%
--   accruing for a CENTURY reaches 3.2e20. There is eight orders of magnitude of
--   slack; this cannot overflow in any real use.
--
-- PATH-INDEPENDENCE — WHY THE "SMALL STEPS VS ONE STEP" QUESTION HAS A CLEAN ANSWER.
-- Accrual is ALWAYS computed from the ATTESTED BASE and the elapsed time since the
-- attested instant. It is never computed from a previously accrued value, so there is
-- no state to carry and no path to be dependent on: `navAt` is a pure function of the
-- clock. Evaluating it at t2 gives the same number whether or not anyone evaluated it
-- at t1 first, EXACTLY. The only discrepancy that can exist is between one evaluation
-- and the SUM OF SEVERAL INDEPENDENT ONES — each of which carries its own half-ulp —
-- so N separately-computed sub-period accruals can differ from the single-step figure
-- by at most N x 5e-11, and by nothing else. A day split into 24 hourly steps is
-- therefore within 1.2e-9 of the one-step number, and `Test:testAccrualStepsAgree`
-- measures it rather than asserting it. THAT IS THE HONEST STATEMENT: the two are not
-- bit-identical, they are identical to within the rounding of the arithmetic itself,
-- and the bound is derived rather than tuned.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
accruedAmount : Decimal -> Decimal -> Text -> Int -> Decimal
accruedAmount basePrice ratePerAnnum dayCount elapsedMicros =
  case dayCountYearMicros dayCount of
    -- An unrecognised convention accrues NOTHING rather than guessing a year length.
    -- Unreachable through the templates (both propose choices reject it up front);
    -- written anyway so the function is total for a direct caller.
    None -> 0.0
    Some yearMicros ->
      if elapsedMicros <= 0
        then 0.0
        else (basePrice * (ratePerAnnum * intToDecimal elapsedMicros))
               / intToDecimal yearMicros

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- navAt — WHAT ONE SHARE IS WORTH AT ANY INSTANT. The headline function.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Total, pure, deterministic: same fixing and same instant give the same Decimal on
-- every validator that re-executes the transaction, which is exactly the property
-- Canton needs and exactly why this cannot live in a backend service.
--
-- FLOORED AT ZERO, and the reason is real rather than paranoid: EUR, CHF and JPY
-- money markets printed NEGATIVE rates from 2015 to 2022, so a fund that accrues
-- DOWNWARD is a thing that has actually existed and `ratePerAnnum` is allowed to be
-- negative. What must never happen is this function handing a negative "price" to a
-- template whose `ensure` demands a positive one. A fixing whose accrual would drive
-- the NAV through zero reports zero — and an auction cannot be anchored on it at all,
-- because `ClosingAuction.ensure referencePrice > 0.0` rejects it outright. The
-- degenerate case fails loudly at the ledger, not quietly in the arithmetic.
navAt : NavFixing -> Time -> Decimal
navAt f at =
  let accrued = accruedAmount f.price f.ratePerAnnum f.dayCount
                  (elapsedMicrosFrom f.accrualFrom at)
  in max 0.0 (f.price + accrued)

-- navAtChecked — the discriminating variant. `None` means "you asked about an instant
-- BEFORE this fixing applies from", which `navAt` answers with the base price and
-- cannot distinguish from "you asked at the strike instant". A reporting or backfill
-- caller that must not silently treat a pre-strike query as an at-strike one uses
-- this; a caller that just needs a number uses `navAt`. Both exist precisely so the
-- clamp in `elapsedMicrosFrom` is a documented choice and not a hidden one.
navAtChecked : NavFixing -> Time -> Optional Decimal
navAtChecked f at =
  if convertRelTimeToMicroseconds (subTime at f.accrualFrom) < 0
    then None
    else Some (navAt f at)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE ANCHOR TOLERANCE — how stale an auction anchor is allowed to be.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `MarketOnClose.RunClose` used to demand `fixing.price == referencePrice` — exact
-- equality with the STRUCK number. Accrual makes that assert wrong rather than
-- strict: the correct anchor for a close at 16:00 is the NAV ACCRUED TO 16:00, which
-- is by construction not equal to a price struck earlier in the day. So the check
-- becomes a consistency band, and the band needs a defensible width.
--
-- ONE BASIS POINT OF THE ACCRUED NAV. Why that number: it is the staleness budget,
-- expressed in the unit the anchor is actually wrong in. At a 3.6%/yr ACT/360
-- money-market rate a fund accrues EXACTLY 1bp per day (0.036 / 360 = 0.0001), so a
-- 1bp band says, in plain terms, "the anchor may be up to one day old". At 4% it is
-- about 21.6 hours; at 5%, 17.3. That is comfortably more than the life of a closing
-- auction and far less than the gap between fixings, which is the window the bound
-- exists to police.
--   NOTE WHAT IT IS NOT: it is NOT a price-integrity control. The collar
--   (`MarketOnClose.collarBand`, 10% or $0.50) is what stops a bad print. This is a
--   STALENESS control on the venue's bookkeeping — it catches a venue anchoring on
--   yesterday's mark, and nothing else. Tightening it to zero would forbid an anchor
--   set even one microsecond before the close, which is every anchor.
--
-- ASYMMETRIC, DELIBERATELY. Being BELOW the accrued NAV is staleness — forgivable up
-- to the budget, because it is what a clock passing between two ledger transactions
-- does on its own. Being ABOVE it is not staleness at all: it is a venue anchoring on
-- value the fund has not earned yet, which no amount of elapsed time explains. So the
-- upper side is not a band, it is a hard edge with only arithmetic slack behind it.
anchorAccrualToleranceBps : Int
anchorAccrualToleranceBps = 1

-- Ten units in the last place. Not an economic allowance — it is there so a caller
-- that reproduces `navAt` through a slightly different (equally valid) sequence of
-- roundings is not rejected over 1e-10 of a cent.
anchorRoundingSlack : Decimal
anchorRoundingSlack = 0.0000000010

-- anchorConsistentWithNav accruedNav anchor — may an auction anchored at `anchor`
-- run against a fixing that says the NAV is `accruedNav` right now?
anchorConsistentWithNav : Decimal -> Decimal -> Bool
anchorConsistentWithNav accruedNav anchor =
  let staleBudget = accruedNav * intToDecimal anchorAccrualToleranceBps / 10000.0
  in anchor <= accruedNav + anchorRoundingSlack        -- never ahead of the accrual
     && accruedNav - anchor <= staleBudget             -- and no more than 1bp behind

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- wrapperConsistent — the wrapper mark must RECONCILE.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- An attested price that does not equal `referencePrice x wrapperFactor` is two
-- different assertions wearing one signature, and the auditor cannot tell which of
-- them the members meant. Either the pair is present and reconciles, or neither is:
-- half a recipe is not a recipe, so `Some`/`None` is refused as firmly as a bad
-- product. Tolerance is 1e-6 relative, which absorbs Decimal rounding on a
-- five-figure mark without admitting a second opinion about the price.
wrapperConsistent : Decimal -> Optional Decimal -> Optional Decimal -> Bool
wrapperConsistent price mRef mFactor = case (mRef, mFactor) of
  (None,   None)   -> True
  (Some r, Some f) -> r > 0.0 && f > 0.0 && abs (price - r * f) <= price / 1000000.0
  _                -> False

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE SIGNER PROTOCOL — what a member ASSERTS, not what it thinks.
-- ===========================================================================
-- docs/SIGNER_PROTOCOL.md. A committee whose members each answer "do you like this
-- price?" is a committee that rubber-stamps, because an opinion costs judgement and
-- these members are not paid (§7 of the methodology). So no member is ever asked for
-- an opinion about the price. Each is asked to assert A FACT ONLY IT CAN SEE:
--
--   issuer  — redemption is working: attestor quorum, reserves current, queue clear
--   lender  — it will carry this mark against its OWN collateral book
--   venue   — the mark sits inside the range its book actually traded
--
-- Two properties follow, and they are the whole point:
--   1. Signing is CHEAP — a check against your own systems, automatable as a bot.
--      That is what makes an unpaid committee operable at all.
--   2. Refusal is SPECIFIC — "attestor quorum was 5 of 10", not "I disagree". A
--      refusal that names a condition is actionable; a vote of no confidence is not.
--
-- `checksPassed` carries the NAMED conditions from the protocol document that the
-- member verified. It is deliberately `[Text]` rather than an enum: the protocol is
-- versioned prose that will gain conditions faster than the DAR is upgraded, and a
-- closed enum would force a package upgrade to add a check.
data SignerCheck = SignerCheck
  with
    member       : Party
    role         : Text              -- "issuer" | "lender" | "venue" | "operator"
    protocolRef  : Text              -- e.g. "SIGNER_PROTOCOL v1 §2 issuer"
    checksPassed : [Text]            -- the named conditions verified
    observedLow  : Optional Decimal  -- venue only: low of the traded range
    observedHigh : Optional Decimal  -- venue only: high of the traded range
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- OperatorCommittee — the standing K-of-N committee (the decentralised operator).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- This is the configuration record: who the member parties are, and how many of
-- them (`threshold`) must attest before a fix is official. `admin` is the party
-- that maintains membership (a coordinator, itself potentially decentralised in a
-- production topology); it does NOT get to strike prices — only members do, and
-- only K-of-N of them together.
template OperatorCommittee
  with
    admin     : Party    -- maintains the committee roster (coordinator)
    members   : [Party]  -- the attestor / operator members
    threshold : Int      -- K: how many DISTINCT members must attest a fix
    auditor   : Party    -- compliance observer of the governance process
    label     : Text     -- human name, e.g. "Lucilla NAV Committee"
  where
    signatory admin
    -- The members and the auditor observe the committee so members can propose into
    -- it and everyone can see the roster/threshold under which fixes are struck.
    observer (auditor :: members)

    ensure threshold > 0
        && threshold <= length members
        && length (dedup members) == length members   -- no duplicate members
        && not (null members)

    -- PROPOSE A FIXING — a member opens a price attestation for an instrument's
    -- open/close. Nonconsuming so the committee runs many fixes off one committee
    -- contract. The proposer is the FIRST approver (it signs by proposing).
    --
    -- THIS IS THE SNAPSHOT PATH, AND IT IS UNCHANGED. Its argument list is exactly
    -- what it always was, deliberately: the Java backends call
    -- `exerciseProposeFixing(proposer, instrumentId, cashInstrument, session, price,
    -- rationale)` positionally through Daml codegen, and adding an argument here
    -- would break two committed, currently-green services for no gain. A fix opened
    -- through this choice is a NON-ACCRUING mark — `ratePerAnnum = 0.0`, day count
    -- "NONE" — so `navAt` returns the struck price at every instant and the semantics
    -- are byte-for-byte the ones this module has always had. Accrual is opt-in, and
    -- `ProposeAccruingFixing` below is how you opt in.
    -- PROPOSE A RESTATEMENT — a member opens a correction to an ALREADY PUBLISHED
    -- fixing (docs/FIXING_METHODOLOGY.md §6). Nonconsuming, like the other proposals.
    --
    -- Opened here on the committee rather than as a choice on the fixing itself,
    -- because a correction must be attested by K of N and only the committee knows
    -- who N are. The proposer is the first approver, exactly as when striking a fix.
    --
    -- The corrected recipe is supplied in full — base, rate, convention, origin —
    -- rather than as a delta, so what each member confirms is the whole replacement
    -- number and not an adjustment they have to compute themselves.
    -- PUBLISH A CESSATION NOTICE — retire this fixing with the §8 window served.
    -- Sits on the committee because that is where a fixing's identity lives (the
    -- roster and threshold it is struck under), even though only the administrator
    -- signs the notice itself. See CessationNotice for why the committee does not.
    nonconsuming choice PublishCessation : ContractId CessationNotice
      with
        instrumentId : Text
        session      : Text
        finalStrike  : Time
        successor    : Optional Text
        reason       : Text
        notifyTo     : [Party]
      controller admin
      do
        now <- getTime
        create CessationNotice with
          admin; auditor; instrumentId; session
          publishedAt = now
          finalStrike; successor; reason
          notifyTo = dedup notifyTo

    nonconsuming choice ProposeRestatement : ContractId RestatementProposal
      with
        proposer     : Party
        supersedes   : ContractId NavFixing   -- the published fixing being corrected
        price        : Decimal                -- the corrected base
        rationale    : Text
        reason       : Text                   -- why the original was wrong; never blank
        ratePerAnnum : Decimal
        dayCount     : Text
        accrualFrom  : Time
      controller proposer
      do
        assertMsg "proposer must be a committee member" (proposer `elem` members)
        assertMsg "a restatement must state why the original was wrong" (reason /= "")
        original <- fetch supersedes
        -- A committee may only correct a fixing struck under ITS OWN quorum. Without
        -- this, one committee could restate another's number.
        assertMsg "this committee did not strike the fixing being restated"
          (original.admin == admin && original.threshold == threshold)
        assertMsg "the correction must not repeat the published price"
          (price /= original.price)
        create RestatementProposal with
          admin; members; threshold; auditor; proposer
          supersedes
          supersededPrice = original.price
          instrumentId = original.instrumentId
          cashInstrument = original.cashInstrument
          session = original.session
          price; rationale; reason
          ratePerAnnum; dayCount; accrualFrom
          -- Disclosure survives a correction: every desk that could read the wrong
          -- number must be able to read the right one, without re-publishing by hand.
          publishTo = original.publishedTo
          approvers = [proposer]
          -- A correction carries the ORIGINAL's wrapper mark and evidence forward by
          -- default; a restatement that also changes the factor sets it explicitly.
          referencePrice = original.referencePrice
          wrapperFactor = original.wrapperFactor
          attestations = original.attestations
          tier = original.tier

    nonconsuming choice ProposeFixing : ContractId FixingProposal
      with
        proposer       : Party    -- the member opening the fix (must be on the committee)
        instrumentId   : Text     -- the asset being priced, e.g. "cETH"
        cashInstrument : Text     -- the unit the price is quoted in, e.g. "USDC"
        session        : Text     -- "Open" | "Close": which fix this is
        price          : Decimal  -- the proposed official price
        rationale      : Text     -- why (e.g. "VWAP of the sealed cross", a source)
      controller proposer
      do
        assertMsg "proposer must be a committee member" (proposer `elem` members)
        assertMsg "price must be positive" (price > 0.0)
        assertMsg "session must be Open or Close" (session == "Open" || session == "Close")
        now <- getTime
        create FixingProposal with
          admin; members; threshold; auditor
          proposer
          instrumentId; cashInstrument; session; price; rationale
          -- A PURE SNAPSHOT: nothing accrues, so the day count cannot matter and the
          -- accrual origin is only there to keep the record shape uniform.
          ratePerAnnum = 0.0; dayCount = "NONE"; accrualFrom = now
          approvers = [proposer]        -- the proposer is the first attestor
          -- No wrapper mark and no protocol evidence on the plain path: this choice
          -- predates both and must keep behaving exactly as it always did.
          referencePrice = None
          wrapperFactor = None
          attestations = None
          tier = Some "committee"

    -- PROPOSE A WRAPPED-ASSET FIXING — the benchmark, and the factor that is the product.
    -- ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- For cBTC, cETH, or anything else that is a CLAIM on an asset priced elsewhere.
    -- The proposer supplies the benchmark print (`referencePrice`, e.g. the CME CF
    -- Bitcoin Reference Rate) and the par ratio the committee is being asked to
    -- attest (`wrapperFactor`). The struck price is their product, computed here
    -- rather than passed in, so the three numbers cannot disagree at birth.
    --
    -- Why this is a separate choice: on the plain path the members attest a price and
    -- the wrapper question never gets asked, which is fine for a native asset and
    -- wrong for a wrapped one. Here the question is unavoidable — an issuer that will
    -- not attest par has to say so by refusing, and the refusal is legible because
    -- the factor is a field rather than a component of a number nobody decomposed.
    nonconsuming choice ProposeWrappedFixing : ContractId FixingProposal
      with
        proposer       : Party
        instrumentId   : Text     -- the WRAPPED asset, e.g. "CBTC"
        cashInstrument : Text
        session        : Text
        benchmarkPrice : Decimal  -- the underlying benchmark print, e.g. CF BRR
        parFactor      : Decimal  -- 1.0 = attested at par; < 1.0 = attested at a discount
        rationale      : Text     -- cite the benchmark and its strike, not an opinion
      controller proposer
      do
        assertMsg "proposer must be a committee member" (proposer `elem` members)
        assertMsg "session must be Open or Close" (session == "Open" || session == "Close")
        assertMsg "reference price must be positive" (benchmarkPrice > 0.0)
        -- A factor at or below zero is not a discount, it is a typo. The upper bound
        -- is deliberately generous rather than 1.0: a wrapper CAN trade above par
        -- (redemption queues cut both ways), and refusing to represent that would
        -- force the committee to lie in the one field built to stop it lying.
        assertMsg "wrapper factor must be greater than 0 and at most 2"
          (parFactor > 0.0 && parFactor <= 2.0)
        assertMsg "rationale must cite the benchmark used" (rationale /= "")
        now <- getTime
        create FixingProposal with
          admin; members; threshold; auditor
          proposer
          instrumentId; cashInstrument; session; rationale
          price = benchmarkPrice * parFactor
          ratePerAnnum = 0.0; dayCount = "NONE"; accrualFrom = now
          approvers = [proposer]
          referencePrice = Some benchmarkPrice
          wrapperFactor = Some parFactor
          attestations = None
          tier = Some "committee"

    -- PROPOSE AN ACCRUING FIXING — attest the INPUTS to a value that keeps moving.
    -- ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- THE FOUR THINGS A HUMAN MUST AGREE, and there are exactly four because
    -- everything else is derivable:
    --   `price`        — the base NAV per share the mark applies from;
    --   `ratePerAnnum` — the accrual rate (0.036 = 3.6%/yr), as a decimal fraction;
    --   `dayCount`     — "ACT/360" or "ACT/365F" (see the header: they differ by
    --                    1.389% on the same rate, so it cannot be assumed);
    --   `accrualFrom`  — the instant the mark applies FROM.
    --
    -- WHY `accrualFrom` IS ATTESTED RATHER THAN TAKEN FROM THE CLOCK. A NAV struck
    -- "as of 16:00" is a fact about 16:00 even if the committee's last member signs
    -- at 16:07 — real fixings are agreed after the moment they describe. Taking
    -- `getTime` would silently mis-date every mark by the length of the committee's
    -- own signing round and quietly under-accrue the fund by exactly that gap. It is
    -- an input a human agrees on, so a human attests it; `NavFixing.finalizedAt`
    -- separately records when the ledger actually saw it, so both facts survive and
    -- the difference between them is auditable.
    --
    -- A NEW CHOICE, NOT A WIDER `ProposeFixing`: adding choices leaves every existing
    -- generated Java call site intact, which is the whole reason the snapshot path
    -- above still reads the way it does.
    nonconsuming choice ProposeAccruingFixing : ContractId FixingProposal
      with
        proposer       : Party
        instrumentId   : Text
        cashInstrument : Text
        session        : Text
        price          : Decimal  -- the BASE NAV the accrual starts from
        rationale      : Text
        ratePerAnnum   : Decimal  -- e.g. 0.036 for 3.6%/yr; may be NEGATIVE (see navAt)
        dayCount       : Text     -- "ACT/360" (USD money market) | "ACT/365F"
        accrualFrom    : Time     -- the instant this mark applies from
      controller proposer
      do
        assertMsg "proposer must be a committee member" (proposer `elem` members)
        assertMsg "base NAV must be positive" (price > 0.0)
        assertMsg "session must be Open or Close" (session == "Open" || session == "Close")
        -- REJECT AN UNKNOWN CONVENTION RATHER THAN DEFAULTING ONE. Silently falling
        -- back to ACT/360 on a typo would mis-accrue a sterling fund by 1.389% of its
        -- yield, forever, with a signed attestation saying otherwise.
        assertMsg "unsupported day-count convention (use ACT/360 or ACT/365F)"
          (supportedDayCount dayCount)
        -- A rate at or below -100%/yr is not a money market, it is a typo.
        assertMsg "accrual rate must be greater than -100% per annum" (ratePerAnnum > -1.0)
        create FixingProposal with
          admin; members; threshold; auditor
          proposer
          instrumentId; cashInstrument; session; price; rationale
          ratePerAnnum; dayCount; accrualFrom
          approvers = [proposer]
          referencePrice = None
          wrapperFactor = None
          attestations = None
          tier = Some "committee"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- FixingProposal — an in-progress price attestation gathering member signatures.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The signature set (`approvers`) GROWS as members Confirm; the contract is signed
-- by exactly the approvers gathered so far. It is visible to the whole committee
-- (so any member can add its confirmation) and the auditor, but to NOBODY else —
-- the price under consideration does not leak to the market before it is official.
template FixingProposal
  with
    admin          : Party
    members        : [Party]
    threshold      : Int
    auditor        : Party
    proposer       : Party    -- who opened it (an approver, and who may finalise)
    instrumentId   : Text
    cashInstrument : Text
    session        : Text
    price          : Decimal
    rationale      : Text
    -- THE ACCRUAL INPUTS, UNDER ATTESTATION. They ride on the proposal so that what
    -- each member CONFIRMS is the whole recipe — base, rate, convention and origin —
    -- not just the base. Confirming a price without the rate that will move it would
    -- be attesting a third of a number.
    ratePerAnnum   : Decimal  -- 0.0 = a non-accruing snapshot (the ProposeFixing path)
    dayCount       : Text     -- "ACT/360" | "ACT/365F" | "NONE" (non-accruing)
    accrualFrom    : Time     -- the instant the mark applies from
    approvers      : [Party]  -- members who have attested so far (grows via Confirm)
    -- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- APPENDED LAST AND ALL OPTIONAL — same upgrade rule as NavFixing's
    -- restatement lineage: a non-Optional field, or one inserted mid-record,
    -- fails NOT_VALID_UPGRADE_PACKAGE and orphans every proposal in flight.
    -- `None` on all three reproduces the pre-existing behaviour exactly.
    -- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- THE WRAPPER MARK — the judgement no benchmark administrator makes.
    -- CF Benchmarks prices BTC. It has never priced cBTC. cBTC is a CLAIM on BTC
    -- held under an attestor multisig, and its value is `BTC x f` where f is the
    -- market's confidence that redemption works. f = 1.0 is not a fact, it is an
    -- ASSERTION — and it is the assertion that has broken every wrapped asset that
    -- ever broke. Recording it explicitly is what makes refusing to sign legible:
    -- the issuer is not disputing the price of Bitcoin, it is declining to attest
    -- par. Carrying `referencePrice` alongside separates the two cleanly: the input
    -- nobody argues about, and the factor that is the entire product.
    referencePrice : Optional Decimal      -- underlying benchmark, e.g. CME CF BRR
    wrapperFactor  : Optional Decimal      -- attested price / referencePrice
    attestations   : Optional [SignerCheck]  -- per-signer protocol evidence
    -- WHICH TIER OF THE WATERFALL PRODUCED THIS, docs/FIXING_METHODOLOGY.md §3.
    -- The methodology requires every published fixing to carry the tier used, and a
    -- number whose provenance is unstated is one a consumer cannot weigh: an auction
    -- uncross and a committee attestation are both official and are NOT the same
    -- evidence. `None` is a fixing struck before the field existed.
    tier           : Optional Text          -- "auction" | "committee"
  where
    -- The approvers gathered so far ALL sign — this is the accumulating multisig.
    signatory approvers
    -- Every committee member (so they can confirm) and the auditor observe it.
    -- Members already in `approvers` are also signatories; Daml dedups the overlap.
    observer (auditor :: members)

    ensure price > 0.0
        && length (dedup approvers) == length approvers   -- an approver signs at most once
        && supportedDayCount dayCount
        && ratePerAnnum > -1.0
        -- The wrapper mark reconciles, or is absent entirely (see wrapperConsistent).
        && wrapperConsistent price referencePrice wrapperFactor
        -- Only a committee member may appear in the protocol evidence, and only once.
        && (case attestations of
              None -> True
              Some sigs ->
                   all (\s -> s.member `elem` members) sigs
                && length (dedup (map (.member) sigs)) == length sigs)
        -- A non-accruing mark must be non-accruing on BOTH fields, so "NONE" can
        -- never sit next to a live rate and quietly accrue on an assumed convention.
        && (dayCount /= "NONE" || ratePerAnnum == 0.0)

    -- CONFIRM — another member adds its attestation. The confirming member controls
    -- the choice (its authority is present), so the successor can add it to the
    -- signatory set; the existing approvers' authority carries because they sign
    -- this contract. One new signature per transaction.
    choice Confirm : ContractId FixingProposal
      with
        member : Party
      controller member
      do
        assertMsg "confirmer must be a committee member" (member `elem` members)
        assertMsg "member has already attested this fix"  (member `notElem` approvers)
        create this with approvers = member :: approvers

    -- CONFIRM WITH CHECKS — the same attestation, plus the protocol evidence.
    -- A SEPARATE choice rather than extra fields on `Confirm`, so the plain path
    -- keeps its exact signature and nothing already integrated has to change.
    -- Behaviourally identical: one new signature per transaction. The difference is
    -- that the resulting fixing can answer WHY each member signed, which is what an
    -- oversight record is and what a signature count alone is not.
    choice ConfirmWithChecks : ContractId FixingProposal
      with
        member : Party
        check  : SignerCheck
      controller member
      do
        assertMsg "confirmer must be a committee member" (member `elem` members)
        assertMsg "member has already attested this fix"  (member `notElem` approvers)
        assertMsg "the evidence must be signed by the confirming member"
          (check.member == member)
        assertMsg "a signer must name at least one condition it verified"
          (not (null check.checksPassed))
        -- A venue asserting a traded range must give a coherent one, and the mark
        -- has to sit inside it. A venue whose own book never printed there is the
        -- single most informative refusal in the protocol; letting it sign anyway
        -- would discard exactly the signal the seat exists to produce.
        case (check.observedLow, check.observedHigh) of
          (Some lo, Some hi) -> do
            assertMsg "observed range is inverted" (lo <= hi)
            assertMsg "attested price sits outside the venue's observed range"
              (price >= lo && price <= hi)
          (None, None) -> pure ()
          _ -> abort "an observed range needs both a low and a high"
        create this with
          approvers    = member :: approvers
          attestations = Some (check :: optional [] identity attestations)

    -- FINALIZE — once the threshold of DISTINCT members has attested, promote the
    -- proposal to an official `NavFixing` signed by all of them. Controlled by the
    -- proposer (any approver would do, since all their authority is on this
    -- contract). `publishTo` are the venues/desks the fix is disclosed to (e.g. the
    -- auction operator that will print at it) — governance without leakage.
    choice FinalizeFixing : ContractId NavFixing
      with
        publishTo : [Party]
      controller proposer
      do
        assertMsg "not enough attestations to finalise the fix"
          (length approvers >= threshold)
        now <- getTime
        create NavFixing with
          attestors = approvers
          admin; auditor; threshold
          instrumentId; cashInstrument; session; price; rationale
          -- The accrual recipe carries through UNCHANGED from the proposal every
          -- member confirmed. Nothing about it is (re)decided here.
          ratePerAnnum; dayCount; accrualFrom
          publishedTo = dedup publishTo
          finalizedAt = now
          -- A first-time fixing supersedes nothing. Only RestatementProposal sets these.
          supersedes = None
          restatementReason = None
          -- The wrapper mark and the protocol evidence carry through UNCHANGED from
          -- the proposal the members confirmed, for the same reason the accrual
          -- recipe does: nothing about what was attested is re-decided at finalise.
          referencePrice; wrapperFactor; attestations
          -- A fixing reached through this module is a COMMITTEE attestation by
          -- definition. Tier 1 is the auction's uncross, which prints elsewhere.
          tier = Some "committee"

    -- WITHDRAW — the proposer abandons an un-finalised fix (e.g. a stale price).
    -- No official fix is produced.
    choice WithdrawFixing : ()
      controller proposer
      do pure ()

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- RestatementProposal — CORRECTING A PUBLISHED FIXING, under the same quorum.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- docs/FIXING_METHODOLOGY.md §6. A benchmark without a correction path is worse than
-- one with a wrong number in it: the number is wrong either way, but with no path the
-- administrator's only options are to leave it or to quietly change it, and quietly
-- changing a published price is the thing an auditor is looking for.
--
-- Deliberately NOT a choice on the fixing that one party can exercise. A restatement
-- carries exactly the authority the original did — the same K of N — because a
-- correction is a new assertion about value, not an erratum. If one signer could
-- restate alone, then one signer could set the number alone, and the K-of-N property
-- the whole product rests on would be decorative.
--
-- The original is NEITHER mutated NOR archived. It was published, and desks may have
-- acted on it, so it stays in the record — provably published, next to the correction
-- that points back at it. Consumers take the fixing that nothing supersedes (or,
-- equivalently and more cheaply, the newest `finalizedAt`). Archiving it is also
-- mechanically impossible, for a reason worth keeping: it would need the ORIGINAL
-- attestors' authority, which would let the members who published a wrong number veto
-- its correction.
--
-- NO TIME WINDOW IS ENFORCED HERE, and that is a decision rather than an omission.
-- The methodology's window is two BUSINESS days; business-day arithmetic needs a
-- holiday calendar this package does not have, and enforcing two CALENDAR days would
-- silently shorten the window across a weekend — refusing a Friday correction on
-- Monday. A wrong window is worse than none, because it fails closed on exactly the
-- error most worth fixing. Both timestamps are on the ledger, so a restatement
-- outside the published window is auditable after the fact, which is the honest place
-- for that check to live.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
template RestatementProposal
  with
    admin           : Party
    members         : [Party]
    threshold       : Int
    auditor         : Party
    proposer        : Party
    -- The fixing being corrected, and the facts copied off it so that what members
    -- confirm is the WHOLE correction — which number, replacing which one, and why.
    supersedes      : ContractId NavFixing
    supersededPrice : Decimal   -- what was published, carried for the audit trail
    instrumentId    : Text
    cashInstrument  : Text
    session         : Text
    price           : Decimal   -- the corrected base
    rationale       : Text
    reason          : Text      -- WHY the original was wrong. Never blank.
    ratePerAnnum    : Decimal
    dayCount        : Text
    accrualFrom     : Time
    publishTo       : [Party]   -- carried from the original so disclosure survives
    approvers       : [Party]
    -- Appended last and Optional, same upgrade rule as everywhere else. Carried off
    -- the fixing being corrected so a restatement republishes the WHOLE record, not a
    -- price with its wrapper mark silently dropped.
    referencePrice  : Optional Decimal
    wrapperFactor   : Optional Decimal
    attestations    : Optional [SignerCheck]
    tier            : Optional Text
  where
    signatory approvers
    observer (auditor :: members)

    ensure price > 0.0
        && length (dedup approvers) == length approvers
        && supportedDayCount dayCount
        && ratePerAnnum > -1.0
        && (dayCount /= "NONE" || ratePerAnnum == 0.0)
        -- A correction with no stated reason is indistinguishable from tampering.
        && reason /= ""

    -- CONFIRM — another member attests the correction. Same accumulating multisig as
    -- a first-time fixing: one new signature per transaction.
    choice ConfirmRestatement : ContractId RestatementProposal
      with
        member : Party
      controller member
      do
        assertMsg "confirmer must be a committee member" (member `elem` members)
        assertMsg "member has already attested this restatement" (member `notElem` approvers)
        create this with approvers = member :: approvers

    -- FINALIZE — with K distinct attestations, archive the superseded fixing and
    -- publish the replacement pointing back at it. One transaction: there is never an
    -- instant at which both numbers are live, nor one at which neither is.
    choice FinalizeRestatement : ContractId NavFixing
      controller proposer
      do
        assertMsg "not enough attestations to finalise the restatement"
          (length approvers >= threshold)
        original <- fetch supersedes
        assertMsg "restatement must correct a fixing for the same instrument"
          (original.instrumentId == instrumentId)
        assertMsg "restatement must correct a fixing for the same session"
          (original.session == session)
        assertMsg "the superseded price on the proposal does not match the fixing"
          (original.price == supersededPrice)
        -- THE ORIGINAL IS NOT ARCHIVED, and that is the correct model twice over.
        --
        -- Mechanically it CANNOT be: archiving a NavFixing needs the authority of ITS
        -- signatories — the attestors who struck it — and a correction is attested by
        -- whichever K members are available now, which may not be the same K. The
        -- ledger refused this and was right to. If archiving were required, then the
        -- very members who published a wrong number could block its correction by
        -- declining to sign, which is the opposite of what a correction path is for.
        --
        -- Financially it should not be either. The wrong number WAS published; desks
        -- may have acted on it. A benchmark restatement does not erase the original
        -- print, it supersedes it — the original stays in the record, provably
        -- published, next to the correction that points at it. That is what
        -- docs/FIXING_METHODOLOGY.md §6 requires: "published as a new record
        -- referencing the original. The original is never deleted or overwritten."
        --
        -- CONSUMER RULE, therefore: the current fixing for an (instrument, session) is
        -- the one that no other fixing supersedes. Reading the newest by
        -- `finalizedAt` gives the same answer and is cheaper.
        now <- getTime
        create NavFixing with
          attestors = approvers
          admin; auditor; threshold
          instrumentId; cashInstrument; session; price; rationale
          ratePerAnnum; dayCount; accrualFrom
          publishedTo = dedup publishTo
          finalizedAt = now
          supersedes = Some supersedes
          restatementReason = Some reason
          referencePrice; wrapperFactor; attestations
          -- A correction is attested the same way the original was.
          tier = Some "committee"

    -- WITHDRAW — abandon a correction before it reaches quorum. The original fixing
    -- is untouched and remains the official number.
    choice WithdrawRestatement : ()
      controller proposer
      do pure ()

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- NavFixing — the OFFICIAL, threshold-attested price. Immutable proof.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Its signatory set IS the attesting members, so its very existence proves that
-- >= K distinct members struck this price together. This is the artifact the
-- auction consumes (see MarketOnClose.RunClose's optional `fixingRef`): a close
-- can be bound to a committee-attested NAV, so the printed price is provably not
-- one venue's unilateral number.
template NavFixing
  with
    attestors      : [Party]  -- the members who signed (>= threshold of them)
    admin          : Party
    auditor        : Party
    threshold      : Int
    instrumentId   : Text
    cashInstrument : Text
    session        : Text     -- "Open" | "Close"
    -- THE BASE, NOT THE ANSWER — the accrual analogue of the auction's demotion of
    -- `referencePrice` from "the price" to "the anchor". `price` is what one share was
    -- worth AT `accrualFrom`. What it is worth NOW is `navAt this now`, and every
    -- consumer should be asking that question instead of reading this field.
    price          : Decimal  -- the attested BASE NAV, as at `accrualFrom`
    rationale      : Text
    -- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- THE ATTESTED ACCRUAL INPUTS. Signed by the same K-of-N that signed the base,
    -- because the signatory set of THIS contract is the attestor list — so "K members
    -- agreed this fund earns 3.6%/yr on ACT/360 from 16:00" is provable by inspecting
    -- the contract, exactly as "K members agreed the base was 100" always was.
    -- The ledger derives every later value from these three and the clock.
    -- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    ratePerAnnum   : Decimal  -- 0.0 = a pure snapshot; the old behaviour, exactly
    dayCount       : Text     -- "ACT/360" | "ACT/365F" | "NONE"
    accrualFrom    : Time     -- the instant the mark applies from (attested, not clocked)
    publishedTo    : [Party]  -- venues/desks disclosed the fix (e.g. the auction operator)
    -- WHEN THE LEDGER SAW IT, which is NOT the same fact as `accrualFrom` and is kept
    -- separately on purpose: a mark struck as of 16:00 and finalised at 16:07 records
    -- both, so the committee's own latency is auditable rather than absorbed.
    finalizedAt    : Time
    -- RESTATEMENT LINEAGE. Optional and appended last, for the Daml upgrade reason:
    -- a non-Optional field, or one inserted mid-record, fails
    -- NOT_VALID_UPGRADE_PACKAGE and orphans every fixing already on a participant.
    --
    -- Set only on a fixing that CORRECTS another (see RestatementProposal). A
    -- consumer that finds `supersedes = Some cid` is holding the current number and
    -- can follow the chain back; the superseded contract is archived, and its archive
    -- is permanent history rather than a deletion. `None` on both means this fixing
    -- has never been corrected, which is the ordinary case.
    supersedes        : Optional (ContractId NavFixing)
    restatementReason : Optional Text
    -- THE WRAPPER MARK AND THE OVERSIGHT RECORD, appended last under the same
    -- upgrade rule as the lineage fields above. `referencePrice` is the input nobody
    -- argues about (CME CF BRR, an index print); `wrapperFactor` is the par ratio the
    -- committee actually decided; `attestations` is WHY each member signed, which is
    -- the difference between a multisig and an oversight function. All `None` is a
    -- fixing struck before the protocol existed, and reads exactly as it always did.
    referencePrice : Optional Decimal
    wrapperFactor  : Optional Decimal
    attestations   : Optional [SignerCheck]
    tier           : Optional Text   -- "auction" | "committee"; §3 waterfall provenance
  where
    signatory attestors
    -- The auditor and the published-to venues observe the official fix; the fix is
    -- deliberately NOT world-readable — it reaches exactly the desks that consume it.
    observer (auditor :: publishedTo)

    ensure price > 0.0
        && threshold > 0
        && length attestors >= threshold          -- provably K-of-N attested
        && length (dedup attestors) == length attestors
        && supportedDayCount dayCount
        && ratePerAnnum > -1.0
        && (dayCount /= "NONE" || ratePerAnnum == 0.0)
        -- The published wrapper mark reconciles, or is absent (see wrapperConsistent).
        && wrapperConsistent price referencePrice wrapperFactor
        -- Evidence, where present, comes only from parties that actually signed.
        && (case attestations of
              None -> True
              Some sigs ->
                   all (\s -> s.member `elem` attestors) sigs
                && length (dedup (map (.member) sigs)) == length sigs)

    -- DISCLOSE — extend the fix to another consuming venue/desk after the fact
    -- (e.g. a second auction that wants to print at the same official NAV). Any
    -- attestor may broaden publication; it never changes the price. Consuming +
    -- re-create because observers are fixed at creation.
    choice PublishTo : ContractId NavFixing
      with
        attestor : Party
        venue    : Party
      controller attestor
      do
        assertMsg "only an attestor may broaden publication" (attestor `elem` attestors)
        create this with publishedTo = venue :: filter (/= venue) publishedTo

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- navPriceOf — read the attested BASE off a fixing (convenience for consumers).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ⚠️ THIS IS THE STRUCK NUMBER, NOT TODAY'S NUMBER. It is the value at
-- `accrualFrom` and it is only the current NAV for a non-accruing fixing
-- (`ratePerAnnum == 0.0`). Any consumer that cares what a share is worth NOW wants
-- `navAt f now`. Kept because it is still the right function for reading what the
-- committee actually signed, and named honestly rather than removed.
navPriceOf : NavFixing -> Decimal
navPriceOf f = f.price

-- ===========================================================================
-- CESSATION — retiring a fixing without stranding the contracts that use it.
-- ===========================================================================
-- docs/FIXING_METHODOLOGY.md §8, which until now was a process with nothing
-- behind it. A benchmark that can simply stop is a benchmark nobody can safely
-- reference: a margin agreement naming "the 16:00 CDX-CBTC-D fixing" needs time
-- to be amended, and the amendment is a negotiation between two institutions,
-- not a config change. Sixty days is the window that makes referencing it a
-- survivable decision.
--
-- WHY THE ADMINISTRATOR SIGNS THIS AND NOT THE COMMITTEE. A restatement is a new
-- assertion about VALUE, so it carries exactly the authority the original did —
-- the same K of N. Cessation asserts nothing about value. It is an operational
-- commitment by the administrator to keep striking until a named date, and the
-- administrator is the only party who can make that promise or be held to it.
-- Requiring committee sign-off would also let members who want the fixing gone
-- block the very notice that protects its consumers.
--
-- THE NOTICE PERIOD IS ENFORCED, unlike the restatement window in §6, and the
-- difference is deliberate. The restatement window is two BUSINESS days, which
-- needs a holiday calendar this package does not carry. Sixty CALENDAR days needs
-- no calendar at all, so there is no reason to leave it to policy — and a
-- cessation notice served short is precisely the failure that strands a contract.

-- The minimum notice, in microseconds. 60 days, per §8.
minCessationNoticeMicros : Int
minCessationNoticeMicros = 60 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000000

template CessationNotice
  with
    admin        : Party
    auditor      : Party
    instrumentId : Text
    session      : Text            -- "Open" | "Close": which fixing is ceasing
    publishedAt  : Time            -- when notice was served
    finalStrike  : Time            -- the last strike this fixing will produce
    successor    : Optional Text   -- a recommended replacement identifier, if one exists
    reason       : Text            -- never blank; a silent withdrawal is the thing §8 prevents
    notifyTo     : [Party]         -- everyone disclosed the fixing, so nobody learns late
  where
    signatory admin
    -- The auditor and every consuming desk observe it. A notice the consumers
    -- cannot see is not a notice, and disclosure is the entire product here.
    observer (auditor :: notifyTo)

    ensure reason /= ""
        && (session == "Open" || session == "Close")
        && instrumentId /= ""
        -- SIXTY DAYS, CHECKED. Serving a short notice would satisfy the letter of
        -- "we announced it" while denying counterparties the window the number was
        -- referenced on the strength of.
        && convertRelTimeToMicroseconds (subTime finalStrike publishedAt)
             >= minCessationNoticeMicros

    -- EXTEND — push the final strike further out. Only ever later, never sooner:
    -- a consumer that has begun amending its contracts against a published date
    -- can absorb more time and cannot absorb less.
    choice ExtendCessation : ContractId CessationNotice
      with
        newFinalStrike : Time
        note           : Text
      controller admin
      do
        assertMsg "a cessation date may only be moved later, never sooner"
          (convertRelTimeToMicroseconds (subTime newFinalStrike finalStrike) > 0)
        assertMsg "an extension must say why" (note /= "")
        create this with finalStrike = newFinalStrike; reason = reason <> " | extended: " <> note

    -- WITHDRAW — the fixing will continue after all. Consuming rather than
    -- archiving-and-forgetting, so the withdrawal is itself an event the observers
    -- see, rather than a notice that quietly vanishes from their view.
    choice WithdrawCessation : ()
      with
        note : Text
      controller admin
      do
        assertMsg "a withdrawal must say why" (note /= "")
        pure ()

-- daysUntilCessation — how long a consumer still has, at a given instant.
-- Negative once the final strike has passed. Exposed because "is this fixing
-- still safe to reference" is the only question a consumer asks of this contract.
daysUntilCessation : CessationNotice -> Time -> Int
daysUntilCessation n at =
  convertRelTimeToMicroseconds (subTime n.finalStrike at) / (24 * 60 * 60 * 1000000)
